    {
      "text": "**Status → Cache → Rerun**\n\nBelow is a quick, generic play‑book that works for most CI/test setups (GitHub Actions, GitLab CI, CircleCI, Jenkins, or a local `pytest` run). Adjust the paths/filenames to match your project.\n\n---\n\n## 1️⃣ Read the latest run status  \n\n| System | Command / UI |\n|--------|---------------|\n| **GitHub Actions** | `gh run list --limit 1`  →  shows the last workflow run ID and its conclusion (`success`, `failure`, `cancelled`). <br>Or open **Actions** tab → most recent run → look at the “Conclusion”. |\n| **GitLab CI** | `curl --header \"PRIVATE-TOKEN: $TOKEN\" \"https://gitlab.com/api/v4/projects/<id>/pipelines?per_page=1\"` → check `status` field. |\n| **CircleCI** | `circleci pipeline list -o json | jq '.[0].status'` |\n| **Jenkins** | Open the job URL → “Last Build” → status badge or console. |\n| **Local (pytest)** | `pytest --last-failed --collect-only` – prints the nodeids of the previously failed tests. |\n\n*If you need the exact logs, grab them now; they’ll help you confirm whether the failures are flaky or permanent.*\n\n---\n\n## 2️⃣ Enable (or improve) the build/test cache  \n\n| System | Where to edit | Minimal snippet |\n|--------|----------------|-----------------|\n| **GitHub Actions** | `.github/workflows/<name>.yml` | ```yaml\\njobs:\\n  build:\\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\\n    steps:\\n      - uses: actions/checkout@v4\\n      - name: Restore Cache\\n        uses: actions/cache@v4\\n        with:\\n          path: ~/.cache/pip\\n          key: ${{ runner.os }}-pip-${{ hashFiles('requirements.txt') }}\\n          restore-keys: |\\n            ${{ runner.os }}-pip-\\n``` |\n| **GitLab CI** | `.gitlab-ci.yml` | ```yaml\\ncache:\\n  paths:\\n    - .pip_cache/\\n``` |\n| **CircleCI** | `.circleci/config.yml` | ```yaml\\nsteps:\\n  - restore_cache:\\n      keys:\\n        - pip-cache-{{ checksum \\\"requirements.txt\\\" }}\\n  - run: pip install -r requirements.txt --cache-dir ~/.cache/pip\\n  - save_cache:\\n      paths:\\n        - ~/.cache/pip\\n      key: pip-cache-{{ checksum \\\"requirements.txt\\\" }}\\n``` |\n| **Jenkins (Declarative)** | `Jenkinsfile` | ```groovy\\noptions { cache(path: '~/.m2/repository') }\\n``` |\n| **Local** | Add `--cache-dir` flag or a `.env` variable | `export XDG_CACHE_HOME=$HOME/.cache` then `pytest --cache-show` to verify. |\n\n*Tip:* Commit the cache config and push **once** – subsequent runs will automatically hit the cache, cutting down on repeated dependency installs.\n\n---\n\n## 3️⃣ Rerun only the failed cases  \n\n| Tool / CI | Command |\n|----------|---------|\n| **GitHub Actions** (workflow dispatch) | Add a **workflow_dispatch** input called `rerun_failed`. In the job, guard the test step: <br>```yaml\\nif: github.event.inputs.rerun_failed == 'true'\\n  run: pytest \\`pytest --last-failed\\`\\n``` <br>Then trigger the run via: <br>`gh workflow run <workflow.yml> -f rerun_failed=true` |\n| **GitLab CI** | Use the built‑in “Retry” button on the failed job, or run a manual job that does: <br>`pytest $(cat .pytest_cache/v/cache/lastfailed)`. |\n| **CircleCI** | Click **Rerun failed jobs** UI button, or via API: <br>`circleci pipeline rerun --pipeline-id <id> --jobs <failed_job>` |\n| **Jenkins** | Click **Replay** on the failed build and add `--last-failed` to the test command. |\n| **Local** | `pytest --last-failed` – runs only the tests that failed in the previous session. <br>For a one‑off rerun without cache: `pytest -x` will stop at first failure, then you can re‑invoke with `--lf`. |\n| **PyTest with cache enabled** | `pytest --failed-first --maxfail=5` – runs failures first, then continues. |\n\n---\n\n## 4️⃣ Quick one‑liner (if you control the environment)\n\n```bash\n# 1️⃣ Grab status\nSTATUS=$(gh run list --limit 1 --json conclusion,number -q '.[0] | \"\\(.conclusion) \\(.number)\"')\necho \"Last run: $STATUS\"\n\n# 2️⃣ Ensure cache (GitHub Actions example – no‑op locally)\n# (just a reminder – cache config lives in the YAML)\n\n# 3️⃣ Rerun failures locally\npytest --last-failed\n```\n\nRun the snippet in your repo’s root. If you’re on a CI platform, replace the `gh` call with the appropriate API/CLI.\n\n---\n\n### What to do next\n\n1. **Confirm the platform** – if you’re not on the ones above, tell me the CI system and I’ll give the exact YAML/command.\n2. **Push the cache config** – commit the snippet, push, and watch the next run hit the cache.\n3. **Rerun** – trigger the “rerun failed” mechanism (UI button or CLI) and monitor the new run for any persisting failures.\n\nLet me know which CI you’re using or if you hit a snag; we’ll tweak the script accordingly. 🚀",
